A water level sensor is a device that detects and measures the height or volume of liquid in a tank, reservoir, or pipe. They are essential for preventing overflows, automating pumps, and managing smart irrigation systems. 

www.renkeer.com
 +4
The three most common types of water sensors vary by application and technology:
Float Sensors: Contact-based mechanical sensors. They use a buoyant, magnetic float attached to a switch that triggers an alert or controls a pump when the water reaches a specific height. 

www.renkeer.com
 +1
Non-Contact (Capacitive/Ultrasonic): These emit sound waves or measure capacitance through the walls of non-metallic tanks or pipes. They require no drilling and are ideal for corrosive liquids. 

www.renkeer.com
 +4
Submersible (Hydrostatic): Dropped directly into the liquid, these calculate the water level based on the pressure exerted by the weight of the water above them. 

www.renkeer.com
For DIY electronics and smart home setups (like Arduino or Raspberry Pi), simple depth-detection modules are highly popular and affordable. For complete, ready-to-use home automation, modern smart monitors (such as radar-based tank sensors) transmit continuous data and inflow/outflow insights directly to your smartphone. 

www.waltr.in
